---
// Pearl › 05 — Building the Engine (dark band, the centerpiece, Figma 195:968): badge, label
// and centred headline, two paragraphs, a three-stat row, then two sub-points ("The harder
// calls", "The harder test") in the reading column.
//
// The band is wide so the stats can span 972; the text is held to the 740 column inside it.
// The nav goes white while this band is behind it (Section tone="dark").
import Section from './Section.astro';

const stats = [
  { value: '8', label: 'Core variants' },
  { value: '90', label: 'Generated' },
  { value: '1', label: 'Edit point' },
];
---

<Section tone="dark" width="wide" labelledby="engine-title">
  <div class="column">
    <div class="heading">
      <span class="text-title-l badge">Centerpiece</span>
      <p class="text-label-m label">Building the engine</p>
      <h2 id="engine-title">
        One base component,<br /> <span class="accent">ninety variants,</span><br /> one edit point
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="lead text-body-xl">
      <p>
        For Pearl, I tried a new way of building components, and it was a risk. Every major
        component started as a single base, with each part switched on or off by a boolean
        property, instead of duplicated instances. The input field shows it best. One base with
        8 core variants grew into roughly 90 variants downstream, covering every style and state.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body-l-mobile">
        The risk paid off mid-project. Our inputs used a traditional layout, with the label
        sitting outside the field, and the client asked for the label to sit on the border
        instead. Rather than updating 90 variants one by one, I changed the base component once,
        and every variant updated with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <dl class="stats">
    {stats.map(({ value, label }) => (
      <div class="stat">
        <dt class="text-label-m">{label}</dt>
        <dd class="text-number-l">{value}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <div class="column">
    <div class="points">
      <section class="point" aria-labelledby="engine-calls">
        <h3 class="text-title-m point-title" id="engine-calls">The harder calls</h3>
        <div class="pair text-body-xl text-body-l-mobile">
          <p>
            Focus states were the hardest call on web. On mobile, an input showed focus by
            thickening its border from 1px to 2px in the active color, with no ring. On web,
            accessibility pointed toward a focus ring, but a ring on input fields added a 2px gap
            that pushed whole forms out of line. Material, Atlassian and eBay all used a ring on
            most components but adjusted the border on inputs, in the same color. So Pearl did the
            same, and I changed the focus color to match the input’s active color so the two never
            clashed.
          </p>
          <div class="stack">
            <p>
              Focus itself needed rethinking once. I first treated it as one of the button states,
              until I realised a user could hover or press while a button was focused. So focus
              became its own true or false property, separate from the rest.
            </p>
            <p>
              Disabled buttons raised one more question. A hard-disabled button loses focus
              completely, because the browser removes it. But a soft-disabled button still shows a
              tooltip on hover, so it can still be focused, and in Pearl it kept its focus ring.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="point" aria-labelledby="engine-test">
        <h3 class="text-title-m point-title" id="engine-test">The harder test</h3>
        <p class="text-body-xl text-body-l-mobile">
          The list component was the hardest test of the model. My first attempt copied the
          mobile variants into web, and the structure broke down. So I started over from just the
          base component and updated its slot instances once. Then came a near-miss. I was about
          to add focus states to every named list type, like list-radio and list-checkbox, before
          I realised that would double the variants all over again. Focus mostly matters for
          documentation and dev handoff in a Figma design system, not day-to-day design, so it
          moved into the base instead, the same discipline the rest of the system relied on.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  /* Narrow reading column (740, no side padding in Figma) centred in the wide band. */
  .column {
    max-width: 740px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    color: var(--text-off-white);
  }

  /* Figma gaps 20, snapped to 24. */
  .heading {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .badge {
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-12);
    border: 1px solid var(--pearl-badge-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--pearl-badge-bg);
    color: var(--pearl-badge-text);
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--text-white);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .accent {
    color: var(--text-pearl-secondary);
  }

  /* Figma gap 18, snapped to 16. */
  .lead {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    margin-top: var(--space-48);
  }

  /* No card on the dark band: three cells across the 972 column, 48 below the text. */
  .stats {
    display: flex;
    margin: var(--space-48) 0 0;
  }

  /* Label sits under the number visually; dt comes first for screen readers. */
  .stat {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column-reverse;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    padding: var(--space-40) var(--space-32);
  }

  dt {
    color: var(--text-off-white);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-pearl);
  }

  .points {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-48);
    margin-top: var(--space-48);
  }

  /* Figma gap 20, snapped to 24. */
  .point {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  /* 6px white dot, 8 before the title (Figma 10, snapped). */
  .point-title {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    color: var(--text-off-white);
  }

  .point-title::before {
    content: '';
    flex: none;
    width: var(--space-6);
    height: var(--space-6);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--fg-white);
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  /* Right column of "The harder calls": two paragraphs, 24 apart. */
  .stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  @media (max-width: 1199px) {
    .stat {
      padding-inline: var(--space-16);
    }
  }

  /* Mobile: badge, label and headline are left-aligned (user); stats and the paragraph
     pair stack. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .heading {
      align-items: flex-start;
      text-align: left;
    }

    .lead {
      margin-top: var(--space-24);
    }

    .stats {
      flex-direction: column;
      margin-top: var(--space-32);
    }

    .stat {
      gap: var(--space-8);
      padding-block: var(--space-24);
    }

    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
